  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "The tune ANTIOCH was arranged by Lowell Mason and published in Boston in the 1830s, appearing in his collections of 1836 and 1839; Mason died in 1872 and Handel, whose Messiah (1741) supplied the opening figures, died in 1759.",
    "sourceEdition": "Mason, tune ANTIOCH in D as printed in nineteenth-century American hymnals; verified note for note and rhythm for rhythm against a public-domain 2/4 ABC transcription of the four-part setting (D. D. Anderson, released to the public domain), with the opening independently confirmed against the Hymnary.org incipit for ANTIOCH (17654 32156 67711), which matches the encoded Verse exactly.",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only; Isaac Watts' 1719 paraphrase is not carried. ANTIOCH is printed in 2/4 and maps cleanly to 4/4 at half the bar rate: each 4/4 bar here is two written 2/4 bars, so the hymnal's 20 measures become 10 bars. No note value is altered by that, and the three sections fall exactly on the tune's own phrase lines. The head begins squarely on the downbeat, so there is no pickup to place. One transcription consulted compresses the phrase-final 'King' to a single 2/4 measure, giving 19 measures rather than 20; the regular four-measures-per-line reading is used here, which is the only place the two differ and it changes no pitch."
  },
